<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · lake</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lake Trasimeno</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Lake Trasimene</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lake Trasimeno is the largest lake of peninsular <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, a shallow lake in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umbria">Umbria</a> near the border with Tuscany, west of Perugia, overlooked by the Etruscan city of Cortona. On its northern shore <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> ambushed and destroyed a Roman army under Gaius Flaminius in 217 BCE, one of the worst Roman defeats of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-punic-war">Second Punic War</a>. Its three islands and lakeside towns are now popular with visitors.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lake-trasimeno.jpg" alt="Lake Trasimeno" width="320" height="191" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Schwarzer Kater from Germany. Color-corrected and cropped by Daniel Case 2008-07-16 · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lago_Trasimeno_wide_view.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
